\subsection{Extended Discussion}
\label{app:extended_discussion}

\paragraph{Depth-graduated curation.}
Current data curation operates on a binary human-vs-machine distinction \cite{dsir2023,qurating2024}.
Recursive generation depth estimation enables a finer-grained alternative: retain low-depth generations (d1) whose distributional properties remain close to the human source, while down-weighting or filtering high-depth text (d3+) where tail erosion has degraded quality.
The 70--85\% in-distribution accuracy and $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$ suggest such depth-graduated filtering is feasible across the model--strategy configurations we study, though its downstream effect on training quality remains to be validated empirically.
Importance weighting \cite{sugiyama2007_importance_weighting} could further enable continuous depth-aware data selection rather than discrete thresholding.

\paragraph{Toward a provenance toolkit.}
Depth estimation complements existing provenance methods along orthogonal axes.
Watermarking \cite{kirchenbauer2023_watermark} provides high-confidence attribution when the generator cooperates but is inapplicable to unwatermarked text.
Source attribution \cite{uchendu2020_attribution} identifies the generating model but not the generation history.
Depth estimation characterizes \emph{iterative reuse} regardless of generator cooperation or identity.
A layered provenance system combining these three signals---watermark presence, source model, and recursive generation depth---could support more nuanced data auditing than any single method.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating four axes: classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and end-to-end learning (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}).

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates three classifier families---bagging (RF), gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM)---on the same 19 features across three atlas cells spanning the full accuracy range.
Per-cell spread is $\leq$0.4pp, establishing a feature-determined ceiling independent of the learning algorithm.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match the within-cell RF of Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} within 0.1\,pp (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected); all feature-based methods significantly outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%, $p<0.001$) and logistic regression (75.0\%).
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); a zero-shot LLM (DeepSeek V3.2) reaches only 36.2\% (95\% CI $[32.0, 40.4]$\%, 500 samples).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Classifier convergence: 4-class accuracy (\%) across three atlas cells. RF (bagging), XGBoost and LightGBM (gradient boosting) operate on the same 19 features; $\Delta_{\max}$: largest per-cell spread. Chance = 25\%. RF values differ from Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} by $\leq$0.7pp due to matched-fold evaluation.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & RF & XGB & LGBM & $\Delta_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc. & 78.8 & 79.0 & 78.9 & 0.2 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$ & 85.8 & 86.2 & 86.2 & 0.4 \\
Pythia, top-$k$ & 68.9 & 68.9 & 68.7 & 0.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Convergence across five structurally different classifiers---spanning bagging, gradient boosting, ordinal regression, and ensemble detection---confirms the signal resides in the features, not the learning algorithm.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Excluding d0, d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.4\% (chance = 33\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
d2 recall varies sharply: nucleus holdouts retain 30--54\%, top-$k$ holdouts fall to 8--19\%, and temperature holdouts span 3.7--39.5\% (median 8.9\%).
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5); per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers it but leaves a residual gap (Limitations).

\subsection{End-to-End Learning}
\label{sec:gen:e2e}

DeBERTa-v3-base \cite{he2021_deberta_v3} and RoBERTa-base \cite{liu2019_roberta} finetuned end-to-end both converge to ${\sim}$59\% (19pp below RF 78.2\%).
The 1.2pp inter-backbone gap rules out architecture bias; Table~\ref{tab:e2e_detail} shows both backbones share the same failure mode---high d0 F1 ($>$0.94) but collapsed d1/d2 performance (F1 $\leq$ 0.42)---while RF achieves 0.596 d2 recall, a $>$2$\times$ advantage at the hardest depth class.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Per-class performance for end-to-end PLM classifiers vs.\ RF baseline (Pythia-1.4B nucleus). PLM columns report F1; RF d2 column reports recall (the class with the largest PLM--RF gap). Both PLM backbones share the same failure pattern: collapsed d1/d2 scores despite adequate d0/d3.}
\label{tab:e2e_detail}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Method & Acc (\%) & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
\midrule
DeBERTa-v3 & 58.3\,$\pm$\,4.7 & .947 & .378 & .247 & .656 \\
RoBERTa & 59.5\,$\pm$\,3.2 & .958 & .416 & .261 & .647 \\
\midrule
RF (19 feat.) & 78.2 & --- & --- & .596$^\dagger$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$Recall; shown for the depth class with the largest PLM--RF gap.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Feature space divergence.}
SHAP TreeExplainer shows perplexity and surprisal features account for 88.8\% of RF's contribution (Kendall's $W{=}0.90$ across three importance measures, $p{<}0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}).
Both PLM backbones correlate \emph{negatively} with RF importance (DeBERTa $\rho{=}{-}0.484$, RoBERTa $\rho{=}{-}0.456$; zero top-5 overlap), with linear probing yielding negative $R^2$ for \texttt{var\_surprisal} and \texttt{p90\_ppl} (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_overlap})---a systematic representational gap, not backbone-specific artifacts.

DCOL \cite{cloc2025} reached 64.5\% (Appendix~\ref{app:dcol_iterations}); all end-to-end approaches (58--65\%) underperform feature-based methods, suggesting depth estimation benefits from explicit tail statistics absent from PLM representations.

\paragraph{Ecological validity.}
While cross-validated accuracy on controlled data is 78.8\%, d0 identification is robust to domain shift: the Pythia-nucleus RF classifies all 546 WikiText-103 test passages as d0 (FPR$\,{=}\,$0\%), despite mean perplexity $+$621\% and variance $+$503\% higher than the training d0 distribution (Table~\ref{tab:calibration}).
This robustness is partly an easy case---WikiText's higher perplexity places it \emph{farther} from d1--d3 in feature space---and does not imply comparable accuracy on wild text at deeper depths.

